maak (0.25.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * (maak python): uv argv builder, output queries and one-line wrappers for
    init, sync, lock, run, add, remove, tree, export, build, publish, venv,
    pip and python; uvx and the interpreter helpers with -m and -I named.
  * (maak python): the pyproject readers answer the name, version, console
    scripts, interpreter constraint and build backend, refusing a version the
    build backend computes rather than guessing it, and python-test, -lint,
    -format, -type-check and -check run the four CI checks through uv run.
  * (maak frontend): package-manager detection from the lock file and a
    parameter that may carry a wrapper, frontend-argv with each manager's
    directory flag, and the wrappers that spell the frozen install, add,
    remove, run, exec and publish differently -- npm, pnpm and Yarn 1 and 2.
  * (maak frontend): the package.json readers answer the name, version,
    scripts and dependencies without starting Node.
  * (maak formats): an array or an inline table may span several lines, and an
    array of tables appends one table per header, so a pyproject.toml that
    configures uv is read rather than refused.
  * docs/python.org and docs/frontend.org; the manual page names MAAK_UV,
    MAAK_UVX, MAAK_PYTHON and MAAK_PACKAGE_MANAGER.

 -- Josep Bigorra <jjbigorra@gmail.com>  Tue, 17 Nov 2026 18:00:00 +0100

maak (0.24.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * (maak maven) and (maak gradle): argv builders for the Maven lifecycle and
    Gradle tasks, with poms, settings, profiles, properties, exclusions,
    threads, daemon/cache/parallel flags and output queries.  Wrapper helpers
    answer with the checkout's mvnw or gradlew when a task runs.
  * (maak elisp): Emacs batch argv, byte-compile, checkdoc and ERT, recursive
    .el discovery, and readers for the Version and Package-Requires headers,
    the provided feature, lexical-binding and the ends-here footer.
    elisp-practices lists the conventions a file is missing.
  * docs/maven-and-gradle.org and docs/emacs-lisp.org; the manual page names
    MAAK_CARGO, MAAK_RUSTUP, MAAK_MAVEN, MAAK_GRADLE and MAAK_EMACS.

 -- Josep Bigorra <jjbigorra@gmail.com>  Mon, 16 Nov 2026 18:00:00 +0100

maak (0.23.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * (maak cargo): Cargo and Rust helpers.  cargo-argv builds the command line,
    cargo runs it, cargo-output returns its output, and the build/check/test/
    run/bench/fmt/clippy/doc/clean/fetch/update/publish/install wrappers are
    one line each.  cargo metadata is read as a value for the package version,
    the workspace's names, the binary targets, the target directory and an
    artefact path.  rustup has argv, output and toolchain/target/component
    helpers.  Programs come from MAAK_CARGO/CARGO and MAAK_RUSTUP/RUSTUP.
  * (maak formats) and (maak declarative): task files in JSON, TOML and YAML,
    read when maak.scm is absent (maak.yaml, maak.yml, maak.toml, maak.json)
    and also usable with -f.  The schema is closed and each reader understands
    a documented subset, so an unsupported construct is an error rather than a
    guess.
  * docs/rust-and-cargo.org and docs/task-files.org; the manual page and the
    CLI, configuration and task pages describe the widened default.

 -- Josep Bigorra <jjbigorra@gmail.com>  Mon, 16 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0100

maak (0.22.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * (maak cc): C and C++ build helpers.  compile/compile-argv,
    compile-objects with #:jobs and #:incremental?, link/link-argv with
    LDFLAGS and link-flags kept apart, static-library, object-file,
    up-to-date?, environment-flags, and pkg-config queries that run even in a
    dry run so --plan shows the real flags.  The compiler, archiver and
    pkg-config program come from MAAK_CC/CC, MAAK_CXX/CXX, MAAK_AR/AR and
    MAAK_PKG_CONFIG.
  * install-file and install-directory in (maak shell), for the install target
    of a Makefile with DESTDIR and modes.
  * docs/c-and-cpp.org: a C and a C++ project, pkg-config, explicit
    incremental builds, a Make/Automake mapping table and the limits.

