maak (0.21.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * `maak package-bundle` passes the bundle name as `#:name`, the keyword
    `(maak bundle)` takes; it was passing it positionally and failed with
    "Invalid keyword: maak" before building anything.  The integration suite
    now runs the task in dry-run.

 -- Josep Bigorra <jjbigorra@gmail.com>  Sun, 15 Nov 2026 13:00:00 +0100

maak (0.21.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * `maak --plan TASK` prints the typed plan a run would execute, without
    executing it: each command, its argv or shell line, its directory, its
    environment and its elevation.  `--plan --json` prints the same plan as a
    versioned object.
  * `maak --watch TASK` re-runs a task when the tree changes, reloading the
    Maak file, the environment files and the starting directory for every run.
    MAAK_WATCH_INTERVAL sets the interval, including fractional seconds.
  * `maak explain TASK` prints the static call graph, the load path, the
    elevation command and which environment file wins for each variable.
  * `(maak bundle)` builds a self-contained Guix pack of a project's
    automation, with a relocatable tarball, squashfs or docker image, so it
    runs where Guile and maak are not installed.  `maak package-bundle` builds
    this repository's.
  * The repository's `hot-reload` and `dev` tasks use maak's own watcher, and
    watchexec is no longer a development dependency.

 -- Josep Bigorra <jjbigorra@gmail.com>  Sun, 15 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0100

maak (0.20.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Every editor integration prompts with the prose the docstring gives an
    argument under its `Args:` line, and shows the tasks a task needs first.
    `maak-tasks --words` ends each line with that prose.
  * Neovim and VS Code forget their cached task list as soon as maak.scm
    changes, so a saved edit is what the next picker shows.  VS Code's task
    provider also fires onDidChangeTasks, which reloads `Tasks: Run Task`.
  * `maak-tasks --executable` and `maak-zed --executable` name the maak a
    generated Zed task runs; MAAK_EXECUTABLE does the same for maak-zed and the
    Helix picker.  The Helix picker no longer shows a default for an optional
    argument that has none.
  * A Homebrew formula builds maak from the release archive and installs the
    maak-tasks and maak-zed helpers, the manual page and the completions.

 -- Josep Bigorra <jjbigorra@gmail.com>  Sun, 15 Nov 2026 10:00:00 +0100

maak (0.19.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The task description is data, and every consumer reads the same one.
    `maak --list --json` prints it as one versioned JSON object: each task's
    docstring, the line its definition starts on, what it needs first, an
    example invocation, and each argument with its kind, default and prose.
  * `task-metadata!` declares what a task is beyond its docstring, in exactly
    three fields: `#:depends`, `#:hidden?` and `#:example`.  A dependency orders
    a run, sequential or -j, and nothing else; nothing is remembered between
    runs.
  * Argument prose comes from an `Args:` section in the docstring, which is the
    convention the library's own docstrings already kept.
  * contrib/bin/maak-tasks is a front end for maak's own reader rather than a
    second parser of the same file, so the helper and `maak --list --json`
    agree byte for byte on a file whose tasks are written out.
  * A parameter is no longer a task: `maak-proc?` no longer counts a value that
    happens to be callable.

 -- Josep Bigorra <jjbigorra@gmail.com>  Sat, 14 Nov 2026 22:00:00 +0100

